On 3/26/23 23:45, Pablo Neira Ayuso wrote:
> You do not have to split your elements in individual commands.
Well, actually I need due to the arising error. My file with set 
elements is generated dynamically and it can be arbitrarily big.

> Could you post some example reproducer?

Sure. Steps to reproduce this issue (files are attached to this letter):
1) # nft -f 1_define_set.nft
2) # nft -f 2_fill_set.nft
3) # nft -cf 3_fill_set.nft

On my machine it is stable to return "internal:0:0-0: Error: Could not 
process rule: File exists" error message with the nftables v1.0.6.

> It would be also good to try with latest nftables version.

I have updated the following packages:
	libnftnl from 1.2.4 to 1.2.5;
	nftables from 1.0.6 to 1.0.7.

libmnl used in both tests was 1.0.5.

After performing update, the error is still present:
> $ nft -V
> nftables v1.0.7 (Old Doc Yak)
>   cli:		readline
>   json:		no
>   minigmp:	no
>   libxtables:	no
> $ sudo nft -f 1_define_set.nft
> $ sudo nft -f 2_fill_set.nft 
> $ sudo nft -cf 3_fill_set.nft 
> internal:0:0-0: Error: Could not process rule: File exists
> 


On 3/26/23 23:48, Pablo Neira Ayuso wrote:
> Also when testing, make sure your -stable kernel contains these fixes:
> 
>    5d235d6ce75c ("netfilter: nft_set_rbtree: skip elements in transaction from garbage collection")
>    c9e6978e2725 ("netfilter: nft_set_rbtree: Switch to node list walk for overlap detection")

I have both fixes already applied.
